<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/intermediate/sampling-and-survey-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You almost never get to measure everyone — you <strong>sample</strong>, and statistics tells
    you exactly how much the sample can be trusted. The central surprise: a well-drawn sample of
    ~1,000 people predicts a nation of 300 million within ±3 points, regardless of population
    size. Precision depends on <strong>n, not on N</strong> — and on the sample being truly
    random.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the 1/√n law — quadrupling the sample only halves the error bar" widget="sliderLab" config={sampleCfg} />

  <p>
    That square root is why polls cost what they cost: halving the margin of error quadruples the
    interviews. It also explains why "we surveyed 10,000 people!" often means little — if the
    10,000 weren't random, no sample size saves you.
  </p>

  <h2>How samples go wrong</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Selection bias:</strong> the 1936 Literary Digest polled 2.4 million readers and predicted the wrong US president — its readers were wealthier than voters. Big and wrong beats small and right, never the reverse.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-response bias:</strong> the people who don't answer differ systematically from those who do. Phone polls miss people without phones; happy customers fill satisfaction surveys.</li>
    <li><strong>Question wording:</strong> "Do you favour helping the poor?" vs "Do you favour expanding welfare?" — same policy, different poll. Professional surveys test alternative wordings and randomise order.</li>
    <li><strong>Stratification and weighting:</strong> sample groups deliberately, then reweight to match the population — the modern fix for uneven response rates (and how election polls recover from their 2016 failures).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Groves et al., Survey Methodology; OpenStax, Introductory Statistics (Sampling and Data).</p>
</TopicPage>
